sequenceDiagram
  %% Target design: one Swamp flow for proof:run (Nick + orchestrator, 2026-11-05). Gateway admits intent; each selected command gets its own parent run on its own proof VM, all in parallel; each run does provision, verify and cancel as children; Gateway projects each run once.
  %% status: draft
  %% command: proof:run
  %% kind: proof.run
  autonumber
  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray
  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue
  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green
  %% lane VM icon=server pill=target tone=amber
  participant CLI as CI job / CLI<br/>orbit proof:run
  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API
  participant SW as Swamp<br/>parent workflow
  participant VM as Proof task VM<br/>nested topology
  CLI->>GW: proof:run COMMIT
  %%> The CI job on a beast or shark runner, or a person, passes only the commit SHA.
  Note over GW: Record the proof<br/>one insert · queue the admission
  %%> Decided by the orchestrator, for Nick's review (2026-11-06):
  %%> - the request records the proof Operation (commit, options, no runs yet), queues the admission job and answers 202 at once; it reads no GitHub, takes no lock and starts nothing
  %%> - why: the request used to do all of the admission and hand the runs to Swamp one after another, which took 40 to 66 s, and a release that reloads PHP-FPM (`process_control_timeout` 30 s) ended it in the middle (proof.run 12343). A queue job is drained by the release with the Horizon workers, which it already waits for
  %%> - the refusals of the admission (`proof.commit_untrusted`, `proof.path_unmapped`, `proof.github_unavailable`, `tasks.capacity_unavailable`, `ai_router.keys_busy`, `proof.execution_disabled`) are now on the proof Operation, with the code and the message, not in the answer of the request; the CLI follows the Operation as before. A request refuses at once only what it can see in the database: `proof.swamp_unavailable`
  %%> - the job runs on the `tasks` queue (three workers), not on `operations`, whose one worker settles every Operation
  GW-->>CLI: 202 Accepted<br/>task ID
  %%> The CLI follows the proof and reads the refusal, the verdicts or the receipt from it.
  Note over GW: Check the commit<br/>orbit branch or approved PR
  %%> Runs in the admission job. Refuses a commit that is not on a hardimpactdev/orbit branch, or an outside PR without review and the run-proofs label.
  Note over GW: Select the cases<br/>diff · selection.txt
  %%> Docs-only changes end here: skipped, nothing to prove.
  Note over GW: Record intent<br/>proof group + full inputs
  %%> Creates the proof task group on Project 46 at the commit and renders every input the run needs. No more Gateway work until the end.
  %%> - the intent of each run also holds `head` and `merge_base`, for the proof that a new case fails on the merge base (stage 3 chart)
  %%> - each run starts with the check of the head (stage 3 chart, "Check the head"): `proof.head_behind_platform` when the head lacks a platform change that main has, `proof.sync_needed` when the proof VM has no `origin/main` to compare with; not built yet
  GW->>SW: Start one run per command<br/>operation-proof-run · parallel
  %%> One PR changes one command, so usually one run. Each run has its own proof VM, as stage 3 says; one failing command does not stop the others.
  %%> Built:
  %%> - the Gateway admits one Operation `proof.command` for each selected command, each on a task group of its own, and Swamp runs the workflow `operation-proof-run` for it; the runs start at once and run in parallel
  %%> - each run records itself once, from its terminal event, and the `proof.run` that the CLI follows closes when the last run has ended
  %%> Decided by the orchestrator, for Nick's review:
  %%> - the CLI follows one Gateway record, `proof.run`, because it needs one task ID; each run is an Operation of its own, so each has one Swamp run, one terminal event and one projection
  %%> - the Gateway closes `proof.run` from the records of the runs and runs nothing; it passes only when every run passed
  %%> - a proof needs the nested fleet, so each proof VM is placed only on a host whose seed holds usable ready copies of the current format (stage 2 chart); with no such host free, admission answers `tasks.capacity_unavailable`, even when a skipped host has free slots
  %%> - the copies must also be fresh: only a host whose ready copies include every change of the playbooks, pins and lock files up to the merge base of the proof commit with main is used (stage 2 chart, decided 2026-11-07); a host whose copies miss one is skipped, and the refusal says so
  %%> - if one run cannot be admitted, for example when no host has room, nothing is kept: all the groups and VMs are admitted in one transaction and roll back together, no Swamp run starts, and `proof.run` fails with the reason
  %%> Decided by the orchestrator, for Nick's review (2026-11-06):
  %%> - a run started with `--report-only` (the report-only proof CI job) yields capacity to task and proof work: its VMs are admitted only on a host that keeps the slots of one large VM free after them, and it never overflows to UpCloud. With less room it fails at admission with `tasks.capacity_unavailable`, whose message names the reserve, and starts nothing. It uses the same admission as every run, with the free slots reduced; no queue and no loop
  %%> - why: two report-only proofs of a Swamp unit change started a run for every case, filled the host and made the Gateway refuse a critical-path proof; the report-only job never has to win capacity
  %%> - the flag goes when the proof CI becomes a required check
  %%> - a report-only VM is always a clone (it claims no ready slot), so a host of 6 slots, such as beast, takes one only when idle (one per idle host); a report-only proof of more commands fails at admission and the job goes red, which is acceptable for a job that is not required
  %%> - the reserve keeps one large VM free; a critical proof of several commands, which admits one large VM for each, can still be refused while report-only VMs run, and nothing queues
  %%> Decided by the top orchestrator, for Nick's review (2026-11-07): a kept VM never blocks a waiting proof. M5 try 10 waited for the VMs of try 9 (kept until 04:35 and 04:37) after their evidence had been read, and A cancelled their groups by hand.
  %%> - rule: when the admission of a proof is refused for capacity (`tasks.capacity_unavailable`) and an earlier proof still holds kept VMs (`--keep-failed`), the admission releases them first. It cancels the group of each kept run, the cancel every run gets with the trigger `proof-run`, and tries again every 20 s until 200 s after the start of the proof, with the step `Wait for VMs to be released`. With no kept VM left, or after 240 s, it fails as before
  %%> - a kept VM stays as long as capacity is not short, so a person who is reading it loses it only when a proof needs the room
  %%> - only a proof that does not yield (no `--report-only`) releases: the report-only job of every pull request meets a full host, and must not destroy the VM somebody is reading
  %%> - all kept VMs go at once, not oldest first: a release takes about 70 s, and the admission has 300 s before the watch ends a proof that has no runs, and its last try may wait 60 s for the admission lock
  %%> - not chosen: release by pull request (the proof knows a commit, not its branch, and a later commit may belong to another pull request), and a release before every admission (it would end every keep at the next proof)
  %%> - the proof that was refused and the proof whose run kept the VM are different proofs; the result of the earlier one still lists the VM, with its time, after it was released
  %%> Decided by the top orchestrator, for Nick's review (2026-11-07): a report-only proof never blocks a normal proof. The reserve of one large VM was not enough: the report-only proof of #3100 (`proof.run` 12688, two runs) was admitted just before M5 try 13, and a full proof needs two large VMs (6 units).
  %%> - rule: the same refusal (`tasks.capacity_unavailable`, a proof that does not yield) also cancels the running report-only proofs, the way a person cancels one (`operation:cancel`): the proof ends cancelled, and the cancel of each of its runs, found by `proof_operation_id`, ends the run, its group and its VM. The admission tries again every 20 s until 200 s after the start of the proof, as for kept VMs, with the step `Wait for VMs to be released`
  %%> - only report-only proofs that have runs are cancelled: one that is still being admitted holds no capacity yet, and if it takes room later the next try cancels it too. A normal proof is never cancelled, and a report-only proof never cancels another one
  %%> - the cancel loses a report-only result that nobody needed to read: the report-only job is not a required check
  %%> - while the VMs are on their way out, the proof waits; once they are gone and the room is still short, it fails with the capacity code as before
  %%> - only the kept runs release: a kept run is a `proof.command` that names its proof and records the VM it kept (`proof_operation_id`), as for the expiry of the keep
  %%> Decided by the orchestrator, for Nick's review (2026-11-06; inferred from the records of the stall of proof.run 12343, with no Gateway log):
  %%> - what happened: the `proof:run` request ended (activity `activity.interrupted`) while it admitted; it overlapped Gateway release 1737. The groups and the `proof.command` Operations had committed, but the request did not start their Swamp runs or write the run list into `proof.run`. The proof stayed `running` with `runs: []`, because nothing watches a `proof.run`, and it held the admission lock until the lock's 600 s limit
  %%> - the runs of a proof are the `proof.command` Operations whose input names the proof (`proof_operation_id`); the Gateway reads, settles and cancels them by that key, never from a list in the proof and never by commit, title or kind
  %%> - the run list is written inside the admission transaction, with the groups, so it commits with them; the transaction first checks that the proof is still open, so a proof that the watchdog ended admits nothing
  %%> - the admission lock is owned by the proof (`proof.run:ID`), so the proof can free it
  %%> - two cases, kept apart. (a) The request is gone: no Swamp run was started and nothing is left to start one. (b) A run waits: it has a Swamp run, which has its own budget, and it is never touched by (a)
  %%> - (a) is detected from the absence of the request, not from the age of a run: a proof with no run 300 s after it started (the job may wait in the queue, the lock wait is 60 s and admission takes about a minute); a proof whose runs have all been quiet for 300 s while some were never handed to Swamp (the job hands them over one after another and each hand-over is answered within 120 s). The reconcile pass then ends the unstarted runs with `proof.admission_interrupted`, cancels their groups, closes the proof and frees the lock the proof owns (a lock that another admission or a teardown holds stays)
  %%> - (b) does not exist before the run is handed to Swamp: capacity is decided at admission and refused at once with `tasks.capacity_unavailable` (`AllocateTaskVmAction::place`, caught in `TaskProvisionIntent::admit`); no scheduler waits for capacity; `capacity_wait_reason` is set only while a parked VM resumes. The generic 600 s admission limit stays as the backstop for an unstarted run and no run is ended earlier because it is slow
  %%> - not changed here: PHP-FPM reload (`gateway-rollback`) waits `process_control_timeout` (30 s) for requests in flight, so a request longer than that dies at a release; the release flow does not drain or reject HTTP admissions
  SW->>VM: Provision the task VM<br/>child workflow · task provision
  %%> The same path every task uses today: clone the task image and seed, boot, enrol, check out the commit.
  SW->>VM: Reset and mount<br/>ready copies · the commit
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - Orbit's own Swamp reaches into the task VM over SSH, like every other step, and resets the nested topology from the ready copies
  %%> - pushes the proof Swamp Club key from its vault into the nested Gateway; the nested Gateway runs its own Swamp as part of the Orbit under test
  %%> - the nested Gateway and app-dev have the VM's checkout of the commit as a disk device (stage 2); `composer install` and `bun install` ran in the VM's checkout; then the apply step: migrations and a restart of the long-running services
  %%> Built, not yet passed live:
  %%> - the reset is the workflow `task-nested-fleet` inside `operation-task-provision` (start from the ready copies, apply, readiness, take `base`); the old direct reset was removed. By 2026-11-06, 12 of 22 fleet starts succeeded; the build of a fleet from scratch (`task_vm.fleet_build`) has not succeeded yet
  opt the commit changes playbooks or pins
    SW->>VM: Converge once<br/>outer VM · nested Nodes
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - image playbooks on the outer VM; role playbooks and pins on the nested Nodes, through the nested Orbit's `node:converge`
    %%> - the nested Orbit reads the playbooks from the mounted checkout; the proof never resets the outer VM, so this lasts the whole run
    %%> - the base images are not touched; only the merge and the seed refresh change them
  end
  SW->>VM: Take base<br/>snapshot
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - a plain Incus snapshot `base` of the three nested containers, with any converged state in it
  %%> - it holds the Club key; accepted because the VM and its snapshots are destroyed after the run
  SW->>VM: Check the workspace commit<br/>HEAD is the commit under proof
  %%> Decided by the top orchestrator, for Nick's review (2026-11-07): before any case the run reads the HEAD of the workspace of its VM and asserts that it is the commit of the proof. Try 13's list run went on after the import of its bundle failed and proved a clone of main
  %%> - a mismatch, or a workspace that prints no commit, fails the run with `proof.workspace_commit_mismatch`, with both commits in the result (`workspace`), and no case runs; the proof ends with the same code when no case ran in any command. A VM that cannot be read at all fails as `proof.run_failed`
  %%> - it runs after the provision, so it is a second line; the gate of the provision is fixed in another lane. See the chart of the kind `proof.command`
  SW->>VM: Run the proof<br/>child flow · proof-fleet verify
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - each batch applies its declared start state; its cases run one after another with their own resources and no undo
  %%> - a batch setup that fails stops the run, and the remaining cases become `unrun`
  %%> - built, not yet passed live: the restore of `base` before each batch and after a case that did not pass is the workflow `proof-fleet-restore`; 0 of 2 runs succeeded by 2026-11-06; the old restore was removed
  %%> - one verdict per case, as in the proof stage of the [command development pipeline](/reference/command-development-pipeline)
  %%> - built: the parent run starts the Swamp workflow `proof-fleet-verify` on Node 209 as a child, with the cases file and the pinned SSH target of this VM; it runs the runner over official SSH, one child per batch and one per case, and no second Swamp runs for this step
  %%> - the parent run joins the verdict lines in its `Report` step; the Gateway reads that step once, when the run ends, and records the verdicts; no step calls back
  SW->>VM: Destroy the task VM<br/>child workflow · always
  %%> Runs after success or failure, so no proof VM is left behind.
  %%> Decided by the top orchestrator, for Nick's review (2026-11-07), so that a person can read a fault that the destroy would hide:
  %%> - `proof:run --keep-failed` (API and MCP: `keep_failed`) keeps the VM of a run that failed, for any reason: a case that failed, a run that failed, or a setup that failed; without it nothing changes
  %%> - decided 2026-11-07 after M5 try 11, whose children failed in `Start the nested fleet` and `Pin the nested CA` inside `Create the VM`: the first version kept only a run whose VM was pinned and whose cases ran, so the faults that need reading most, those of the infrastructure, were destroyed
  %%> - the step is skipped by its guard (Swamp skips a step whose guard is true) when the flag is set and the run did not succeed in every part: the VM was created, pinned, the cases ran and every case passed. A run that passes destroys its VM as before
  %%> - what is kept is the VM that the group has at that time, whatever its state: a VM that failed to provision is kept as it is, and a run that never got a VM keeps nothing, so its group is cancelled the normal way
  %%> - the Gateway then does not cancel the group. It records the VM (name, group, keep-until time) on the run and on the result of the proof, and the proof ends as usual
  %%> - only the groups of this proof run (`proof_operation_id`) are kept. A kept VM holds real CPU and memory on its host whatever its record says, so the capacity of an Incus host counts every kept VM, also one in the state `failed` (decided 2026-11-07: over-committing a host causes slow-exec faults). A failed VM that no proof kept is not counted, because the normal cleanup removes it at once
  %%> - a kept VM stops counting when its group is cancelled and the VM is destroyed, so the release of kept VMs for a waiting proof frees its slot
  %%> - when the group of a kept VM is cancelled and its cleanup is complete, by the expiry, by the release for a waiting proof or by a person (`tasks:cancel`), the kept record on the run and on the result of the proof gets `released_at` and `released_by` (the operation of the cancel). A kept list entry without them is a VM that is still there
  %%> - the keep lasts 60 minutes from the end of the run. The one-minute pass that watches proofs (`ReconcileSwampOperations`) cancels the group when the time is up, with the trigger `proof-run`, and asks again every 5 minutes. It needs nobody to come back
  %%> - a cancel of the proof leaves a kept VM until its time is up; a keep that is still open a day after its time is no longer asked, and the Gateway logs a warning
  %%> Built:
  %%> - the child `operation-task-cancel` with the input that the Gateway captured with the VM at admission; if it did not run or failed, the Gateway cancels the group the normal way, which destroys the VM
  %%> - the Gateway cancels the group of a run with the trigger `proof-run`; a group is cancelled only through the Operation that owns it (its `proof.command`, or its image refresh with the trigger `image-refresh`)
  alt every case of every command passes
    SW-->>GW: Run succeeded<br/>verdicts + receipt
    Note over GW: Record the result once<br/>PASS
  else a case fails or did not run
    SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>failed case
    Note over GW: Record the result once<br/>FAIL
  end
  loop until the run ends
    CLI->>GW: operation:show TASK
    GW-->>CLI: status + verdicts
  end
  %%> The CI job passes or fails on the verdict; the merge requires it, and the proof does not run again after the merge.
